Here's a simple example of a Todo App in Java using Java 8 features. This app will allow you to add, remove, and mark as completed tasks.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// Define the TodoItem class
class TodoItem {
    private String task;
    private boolean isCompleted;

    public TodoItem(String task) {
        this.task = task;
        this.isCompleted = false;
    }

    // Getters and setters
    public String getTask() {
        return task;
    }

    public void setTask(String task) {
        this.task = task;
    }

    public boolean isCompleted() {
        return isCompleted;
    }

    public void setCompleted(boolean completed) {
        isCompleted = completed;
    }
}

// Define the TodoApp class
class TodoApp {
    private List<TodoItem> todoList;
    private int nextTaskId;

    public TodoApp() {
        todoList = new ArrayList<>();
        nextTaskId = 1;
    }

    // Add a new task to the todo list
    public void addTask(String task) {
        TodoItem todoItem = new TodoItem(task);
        todoList.add(todoItem);
    }

    // Remove a task from the todo list
    public void removeTask(int taskId) {
        for (TodoItem todoItem : todoList) {
            if (todoItem.getTask().equals(String.valueOf(taskId))) {
                todoList.remove(todoItem);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Mark a task as completed
    public void markTaskCompleted(int taskId) {
        for (TodoItem todoItem : todoList) {
            if (todoItem.getTask().equals(String.valueOf(taskId))) {
                todoItem.setCompleted(true);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Get the next available task id
    public int getNextTaskId() {
        return nextTaskId++;
    }

    // Print the todo list
    public void printTodoList() {
        System.out.println("Todo List:");
        for (TodoItem todoItem : todoList) {
            System.out.println(todoItem.getTask() + ": " + (todoItem.isCompleted() ? "Completed" : "Not Completed"));
        }
    }
}

// Main program
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TodoApp todoApp = new TodoApp();

        // Add tasks
        todoApp.addTask("Buy milk");
        todoApp.addTask("Walk the dog");

        // Print the todo list
        System.out.println("Current Todo List:");
        todoApp.printTodoList();

        // Mark a task as completed
        todoApp.markTaskCompleted(1);

        // Print the todo list
        System.out.println("Updated Todo List:");
        todoApp.printTodoList();
    }
}
```

This code will create a Todo App that allows you to add, remove, and mark as completed tasks. The tasks are stored in a list and the next available task id is tracked. The `TodoApp` class has methods for adding tasks, removing tasks, marking tasks as completed, and printing the todo list. The `Main` class demonstrates how to use the `TodoApp` class.

Here's a basic example of inheritance in Java:

```java
// Animal class (parent)
public class Animal {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Animal(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating.");
    }

    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println(name + " is sleeping.");
    }
}

// Dog class (child)
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, int age, String breed) {
        super(name, age); // calls Animal constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " is barking.");
    }
}

// Cat class (child)
public class Cat extends Animal {
    private String color;

    public Cat(String name, int age, String color) {
        super(name, age); // calls Animal constructor
        this.color = color;
    }

    public void meow() {
        System.out.println(name + " is meowing.");
    }
}
```

In this example, the `Dog` and `Cat` classes inherit the properties and behavior of the `Animal` class using the `extends` keyword. They also add their own specific attributes and methods. The `super` keyword is used to call the parent class's constructor from the child class's constructor.

You can create instances of the `Dog` and `Cat` classes and call their methods to demonstrate the inheritance pattern.

Here's a simple example of a Quick Short Array (QSA) in Java:

```java
public class QuickShortArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Declare and initialize an array
        int[] array = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80, 90};

        // Print the array
        System.out.println("Array: ");
        for (int i : array) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        // Use a for-each loop to iterate over the array
        System.out.println("Using for-each loop:");
        for (int i : array) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        // Use a while loop to iterate over the array
        System.out.println("Using while loop:");
        int index = 0;
        while (index < array.length) {
            System.out.print(array[index] + " ");
            index++;
        }
        System.out.println();

        // Use Java 8's stream API to iterate over the array
        System.out.println("Using Java 8's stream API:");
        array.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

This code creates an array with 9 elements, prints it, and then uses different methods to iterate over the array. The output will be:

